Each vertex of the polygon could be the vertex of two triangles.

For the general case, for both $A_1$ and $A_2$ to lie on the same side of the line $A_6A_7A_8A_9$, there are 8 possible choices for each vertex, for a total of $8^2 = 64$ ways to place $A_1$ and $A_2$.

Since there are 4 pairs of antipodal vertices on the outside of the polygon, 2 choices for $A_1$ and 2 for $A_2$ cause $A_1$ and $A_2$ to be on opposite sides, leaving the 8 (64 minus the 4) ways to choose $A_1$ and $A_2$.


For the second case of 1 vertex on one side, and 2 on the other, we have 4 ways to choose $A_1$, but only two ways for the vertices on the other side (with $A_1$ and the more ambiguous pair): 


$A_5$ will have to be its own vertex, etc. 

Left with 5 pairs of antipodal vertices (including the 2 we removed in the case above), that's left with 5 ways, but out of 8 possible choices for the pair on the end, when considering the pair on the same side, only 4 choices for this end work.


$8^2 \times 4 - 5 \times 4 \times 3 = 200 - 60 = 140$ 


The $8^2$ comes from $A_1$ and $A_2$ both lying on the same side, and $A_1$ and $A_2$ both lying on opposite sides. This gives us 160 ways for the common vertex to be either 1 of the 4 pairs, or 2 of the 4. 


$6 \times 160 + 20 = 960 + 20 = 980$, the 6 for the first case is $(A_1, A_5)$, $(A_2, A_6)$, $(A_3, A_7)$, $(A_4, A_8)$, and $(A_5, A_{0})$.

